<description>
Drop the columns of the top marker downward, stopping blue-ended columns on grey obstacles and letting maroon-ended columns pass to the bottom.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- The most numerous non-background color forms stationary grey obstacle pixels.
- The other two colors form a single 3x3 top-edge marker whose top two rows
  are maroon and whose bottom row contains maroon or blue control pixels.

Rule
- Erase the top marker.
- Treat each marker column as a vertical drop retaining its three colors.
- A column whose bottom control pixel is blue stops immediately above the
  first grey pixel below that column, or bottom-aligns if none exists.
- Contiguous maroon-ended columns pass through obstacles and bottom-align;
  if such a run has a clear down-right slide from a grey ledge, shift the
  run one column right before bottom-aligning.
- When a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear
  the rest of the marker-width gap in that bottom bar.

Key insight
- The bottom row of the top marker is a control strip: blue cells mean
  "stick to the first obstacle," while maroon cells mean "fall through."
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "columns of the 3x3 maroon/blue marker on the top edge; the bottom cell of each column is the control pixel",
  "path": "vertical drop in each marker column; blue-ended columns stop above the first grey obstacle below, while maroon-ended runs pass through to the bottom with a one-column right slide from a clear grey ledge",
  "trajectory": "copy each three-pixel marker column intact; contiguous maroon-ended columns are tested as one run for the slide",
  "dst": "settled positions on the same canvas after erasing the top marker",
  "transform": "if a maroon-ended drop lands through a grey bar on the bottom edge, clear the rest of the marker-width gap in that bar; leave other grey obstacles fixed"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 14-16 rows x 14-16 cols, square [observed]
  colors: bg=7(orange), obstacle=5(grey), pass=9(maroon), stop=1(blue) -- fixed across examples
  marker:
    shape: 3x3, top-left at row 0, column varies freely within grid width
    structure: top 2 rows all pass_color(9); bottom row is control strip mixing stop(1) and pass(9)
    control_patterns: 1-3 stop cols + 0-2 pass cols; at least 1 stop col
    must not overlap any obstacle pixel
  obstacles:
    pixel_count: 8-12% of grid area (21-31 pixels)
    components: 7-9 connected components (4-conn), sizes range 1-11
    placement: scattered across grid; may appear in rows 0-2 outside marker cols; row 3 may have obstacles
    stop_col_constraint: each stop column's drop zone (3 cells above first obstacle or at grid bottom) must be all-bg
    stop_col_first_obs: first obstacle >=3 rows below marker bottom (landing_top >= 0)
  pass_columns:
    bottom-align at row H-3; contiguous pass runs may slide 1 col right if an obstacle with a horizontal neighbor exists below them AND the shifted 3-tall landing zone is clear
    bottom_edge_gap: if pass col lands through an obstacle bar spanning >= marker_w on the bottom row, the interior of that bar under the marker width is cleared to bg
generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid H,W in [14,16]. Pick marker column c0 in [0, W-3].
    2. Choose a control pattern for the 3-cell bottom row (1-3 stops, 0-2 passes).
    3. Build the marker (top 2 rows = 9, bottom row per control).
    4. For each stop column, choose a landing row (first_obstacle_row in [6, H-1]) ensuring landing_top = first_obs - 3 >= 3. Place an obstacle pixel at (first_obs, c0+j). Ensure the 3 cells above first_obs in that column are bg.
    5. Scatter 7-9 obstacle components (total 20-31 pixels, various shapes 1-11 px) across the grid, avoiding the marker region and stop-column drop zones.
    6. Optionally arrange one obstacle with a horizontal neighbor below a pass-column run with the shifted column clear, to trigger the slide-right behavior.
    7. Verify solver produces valid output (no assertion failures).
```
